  "body": "What happens: `multiaxis::axis::fit` (crates/motion/src/multiaxis/axis.rs) refuses when both smaller eigenvalues of the area-weighted normal moment exceed `ISO = 0.5` times the largest. A plain r10 x 40 mm bar (L/D = 2) sits right on that threshold: the end caps' Z-normal area 2*pi*R^2 = 628.3 equals 0.5 * pi*R*h = 628.3. Model one cross hole into it and the balance tips: the hole wall adds normals in the YZ plane (raising the Z entry), the mouth removes face area, and the part is refused with \"every axis fits this model equally - it curves the same the world around\". The lathe's cross drill (`cross_spindle::lathe_reads`) and axial drill (`axial_doors`) then refuse a real turned part.\n\nFound while fixing #117. #117 now refuses a cross hole the model lacks, so the cross-drill fixtures had to model their holes. On the r10 x 40 bar every lathe case was refused by the fit (drill_reach, drilling_cross_box, drilling_cross_spindle, drilling_cross_stock, lathe_cross_drill). For now the #117 fixtures use a longer bar to stay off the knife-edge; the reader itself is untouched.\n\nRelated: #113 (a large keyway or flat still refuses the part) and #116 (the robust inlier band is far wider than the spindle tolerance) cover the same fit.\n\nExpected: a turned bar with keyways, flats or cross holes reads as turned about its real axis whatever its L/D. The isotropy door should judge the revolved surface (the robust inliers), not the raw normal moment that end caps and holes skew.\n\nDone means: an r10 x 40 bar with one modelled radial hole (for example the `punched_bar` fixture in crates/app/tests/punched_bar) reads as turned about Z on a lathe, pinned by a test; a sphere-like part is still refused.\n",
